<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Omurtag</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/814">814</a>–831</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Omortag</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Omurtag was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> from 814 to 831. He concluded a thirty-year peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> around 815, fixing the border in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> over the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the 820s, and built palaces and fortifications at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> and on the Danube. His many Greek inscriptions are major sources for early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omurtag.jpg" alt="Omurtag" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omurtag1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Omurtag, also known as Murtag or Murtagon, bore the title Kanasubigi and is known as the Builder. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Khan Krum</a> there was a short period of political instability. Byzantine sources name three nobles, Dukum, Ditzeng and Tsog, as persecutors of Christians, and Dukum may be identical with Omurtag. They were either Krum's generals with a large part in government but without the throne, or regents to the infant Omurtag. Historians usually accept Vasil Gyuzelev's view that Omurtag succeeded his father after short disturbances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the summer of 814 the Byzantine Emperor Leo V the Armenian attacked the Bulgarian troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and won a battle near Burtodizos, probably modern Babaeski; Omurtag escaped on a swift horse. News of a possible alliance between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> led to a 30-year peace treaty with the Byzantine Empire in 815, partly inscribed on a column found near Seltsi. It fixed the border in Thrace from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast near Debelt to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> river, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a>, and provided for exchanges of prisoners. It was renewed in 820 under Michael II, and Omurtag later sent an army to help Michael II against the rebel Thomas the Slav.</p><p>In 818 the Slavic Timočani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Abodrites</a> and Braničevci rebelled against Bulgarian rule and sought the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>. After diplomacy failed, Omurtag issued an ultimatum in 826 and in 827 sent a fleet along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a>, which restored Bulgarian control over parts of southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. At home he ended the autonomy of the Slavic tribes and divided the state into large provinces called comitati, whose governors, appointed by the Kanasubigi, held military and civil authority. He also repressed Christians, especially the Byzantine prisoners settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a>, and disinherited his eldest son Enravota, who was sympathetic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Omurtag undertook large-scale construction to restore his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a>, destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 811, and to develop regional centres, palaces and fortifications. Inscriptions at Chatalar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> record the rebuilding of Pliska, a new palace with a large throne room and a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangra">Tangra</a>. An alley of stone columns bore the names of conquered towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. His Tarnovo Inscription reads in part: Let the one who comes later upon seeing this inscription remember the one who had made it. And the name is Omurtag, Kanasubigi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Omurtag of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Omurtag</b> (814–831) made a thirty-year peace with Byzantium and built palaces and fortifications.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Bulgarian Empire under <b>Omurtag</b> reasserted control over the middle Danube in the 820s, and the Timok valley remained Bulgarian.</blockquote><a href="/state/timocani">Timočani</a></li></ul></section>
